Dirk Behrens (behrens@tee.uni-essen.de) wrote:

: mkisofs -o alpha.raw -lrTv -V RedHat-6.0-Alpha -b milo/images/xxxx.img
: alpha/

: You probably don't need the boot-image (-b) option on the
: alpha-distribution since milo might not work with it, but maybe it does in
: your case.

That is kinda funny as -b option creates something which works with BIOS
on Intel machines (i.e. only those versions which conform to newer
specs) which happens to have ATAPI CD drives - not even SCSI, and not
mentioning some older CD interfaces.  Milo is not coming into a picture
at all and this will not work as firmware and boot procedures on Alpha
are different than those on Intel.

What happens is that -b writes on CD an image of an Intel boot floppy
and BIOS loads that into a memory and "cheats" your machine into
thinking that it boots from that floppy.  This is not overly precise but
support for other kind of boot images is often buggy and unreliable in
many currently available BIOS variants; one has a pretty good chance
that 2.88M ones are correctly supported, and not only 1.44M, but beyond
that this is a gamble.

All in all this particular option does not have big prospects on
Alpha. :-) Moreover it is not even needed.  With something conforming to
ARC BIOS specs (yes, there is something like that) you setup a default
menu entry and a non-zero timeout and it autoboots ANY kind of media
provided that needed loaders are available on these (and what they are
you specify in this menu entry).  If you happen to use SRM console this
is a bit different kettle of fish as you need so called disklabel and a
"bootsector" which will be recognized by this firmware.  Distribution CDs
for Tru64 (a.k.a Digital Unix, a.k.a. OSF/1) or for OpenVMS are formatted
that way.

  Michal



