Dirk Behrens (behrens@tee.uni-essen.de) wrote:
: Up to now I always created i386 bootable CDs which works great, also with SCSI

Yes, but that means that your controller have its own BIOS which
supports booting.  Some do.  I was not writing a treatise on boot
variations and all possible options on Intel based machines.  You
want more of that then talk to Andreas Brouwer (dwguest@win.tue.nl)
and your head will explode. :-)

: (I have an Asus board with the NCR controller and included BIOS :) ). What about
: the boot options in the Adaptec SCSI menu?

What about it?  This is not for Alpha.

: By the way, the -b option doesn't create a boot image

I did not say that; at least not deliberately.  Your, correct, command
example which creates a bootable (on Intel) CD makes it clear that you
are grabbing some boot floppy image which was first created elsewhere.

: Anyway, using the -b option or not, mkisofs should at any rate provide a
: workable/bootable RedHat-6.0 CD and that was the question here, wasn't it?

That is right.  This image does not harm anything at all - just takes a
bit of space on your CD.   Of course resulting CD will not boot on
Intel, as this is not an image of a diskette which would be recognized
by an Intel BIOS as a bootable one, nor on Alpha - as ARC BIOS does not
know anything about milo or, in general, about booting that way.

Of course if you have ARC BIOS and somewhere on your CD the first stage
loader (linload.exe in our case) and a correct osloader (some version
of milo for Linux)  then you may edit your menu entries in ARC BIOS
to autoload that setup.  Most people reserve that for a hard drive,
as you can autoboot only one entry, but it is feasible to autostart a
machine that way (put the next stage boot command into OSLOADOPTIONS
when editing ARC); the only catch is that you would have to have a
correct CD in a drive always when autobooting.

