I've had my Alpha box running for longer than that, and Linux/Alpha was
hardly new then.  I'd guess closer to 4 years.

>My question:  Would someone please point me toward a reputable source that
>can clear up this 64-bit Linux question?
>
>I have clients that want to know if they can run Linux instead of Tru64
>Unix, but still get the 64-bit processor throughput.
>
>Any _reputable_ light that can be shed on this topic will be greatly
>appreciated.

The source code is readily available, and is presumably pretty
authoritative.

There are about three main issues con- Linux on Alpha.

a) GCC does not generate nearly as fast code as does Digital's compiler.

Work is certainly going into remedying this situation, but it still
appears to be true.

b) The Digital UNIX libraries appear to be better optimized than GLIBC.

Again, with ongoing "improvement efforts" to diminish the difference,
but this is still a disadvantage for Linux.

c) Digital UNIX has things like AdvFS, *highly* scalable SMP support, and
the like that Linux just doesn't have (yet?).  DU seems to collect data
from devices on "device faults" that can be used to help do preventative
maintenance, and Linux doesn't have that (yet).

Some of these things are "in progress" on Linux, but aren't there yet.

There thus certainly are places where DU can "clean Linux's clock"
in terms of performance and reliability.

But if your issue is whether there is suitable support for the 64
bittedness of the Alpha architecture, then the answer is certainly "Yes,
Linux does that."  They may not get *AdvFS* throughput.  They may not
be able to do SMP right now.  

But those aren't issues that relate to "64 bits."  

<sarcasm>
If your clients are primarily worried about making sure they have "as
many bits as the other IS managers," then there is no reason to consider
Digital UNIX to be preferable to Linux.
</sarcasm>

I don't think I'm being sarcastic particularly invalidly; if those
clients actually have a forcible reason to be worried about how many
bits there are here and there, both you and they should be looking at
the Linux kernel sources as well as the GCC code generator for Alpha
for authoritative answers, and if you can't, then all you'll be able
to get from anyone else are either vague platitudes or "warm fuzzies"
to the effect that "Sure!  We found that our systems run pretty well!"

If neither you nor they are up to the technical task of looking at
/usr/src/linux/arch/alpha, then the number of bits doesn't much matter;
you should *really* be asking questions about the applications they
expect to run on the system, and then determine which CPU is running
that application best.
