: It's older.  Early on there were two independent ports taking place, one
: was 32-bit and the other was 64-bit.

Well, no, not really.  At the beginning of 1994 Jim Paradis from DEC had
the first running trial implementation of Linux which was indeed 32-bit.
This was never distributed to the public.  Short time after that Linus
got involved in the port (Jon "maddog" Hall, also then from DEC - now
with VA Research and Linux International, was instrumental in getting an
Alpha on loan to Linus).  32-bit port got scrapped never to be revived
again and everybody involved was working on the same Linux/Alpha even
if they were not physically at the same place.

: The 32-bit port (BLADE?) disappeared
: long ago.

BLADE, which showed up in 1994 and was available to everybody brave
or foolish enough to try it was 64-bit.  In 1995 on Atlanta Linux
Showcase exhibition there was a Linux/Alpha machine running X-windows.
Things started to move quite a bit faster afterwards. :-)

: I don't remember many details, but I read all about it in LJ... check
: backissues if you're curious.

There was an interview with Jim Paradis in one of the issues.

