On Sun, 20 Jun 1999, Helmut Katzgraber wrote:
>NOTE: i tried it the other way around: mounting othermachine onto
>thismachine and that works. it seems that it is this machine which has
>problems "accepting" the exports file.
>
>thanks, h.

Hi -- just a couple of things to try here --

First of all make sure nfsd and mountd (at least) are registered with the
portmapper -- rpcinfo -p.

Then, from the server, showmount -e.

If registered with portmapper, but still no exports showing with showmount -e,
then kill nfsd and mountd, and start nfsd, and mountd, using the debug flag for
mountd (strrange... I dont have a man page for mountd or rpc.mountd).  mountd
is what reads the exports file.

If you are running redhat, and when booting it tries to start nfs service and
it says 'service unavailable", then you need to build nfs server into your
kernel.

If all shows well at the server machine, then, from the client, showmount -e
servername and rpcinfo -p servername will tell you if your client can see what
it should.

Hope this helps...

Duane




