Well Carlo, here are a couple of things I learned building mine:

1) Get the motherboard manual. It's on the web, it's very helpful.

2) Get some parity RAM. I reccomend 32 MB, the board won't work with less
than 2X4mb parity (36 bit) ram.

3) Get some cache. You can salvage it from old 486 boards, if the numbers
are right. We pick them up here for about $5.00, very used. The MLB manual
lists all the numbers that work.

4) The motherboard fits in an ATX case reasonably well, however, there is no
"pass through" for the soft power connection. I bought a case with no power
and used an AT power supply. You don't get 3.3 volts for the PCI bus, but
not too many PCI cards use the 3.3V. Some ethernet cards are exceptions to
this.

5) It won't boot from an IDE drive, although there is built in support for
IDE drives. You'll need a SCSI boot drive. The embeded SCSI controller is
supported fairly well in RH 5.2 and 6.0.

Well, taht's prolly more than you ever wanted to hear in one message.

Flame suit on!
The Wolfster

