hvdkooij@bastion.nl3155vj16.vanderkooij.org <hvdkooij@bastion.nl3155vj16.vanderkooij.org> wrote:
>Aravind Mikkilineni (mikkilineni.1@osu.edu) wrote:
[disappointed with 'rpm' package management]
>If you are disappointed in general with package management then Debian will
>just be as disappointing.

Package management is a broad concept, just because one instance doesn't
suite you doesn't mean another one won't either.

Debian's package format is quite different from RPM (e.g. it has package
dependencies, not file dependencies; and degrees of dependency (Pre-Depends;
Depends; Recommends; Suggests); see http://kitenet.net/~joey/pkg-comp/).

Debian's approach to building packages is also different from the RPM world.
In the RPM world, you have a small number of distributer's packages (e.g.
Red Hat's), and a large body of contributed packages with no guarantee of
quality.
With Debian, there are virtually no second class packages; everything is
provided by the distribution maintainers.

Ray
