Organization: Electronics Lab, ETH Zurich, Switzerland
MIME-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

tesseract@my-deja.com wrote:
> 
>   It appears that GDB cannot read core files created on Alpha Linux.  Is
> this a known problem that is being worked on, or am I just doing
> something wrong?

In Alpha Linux 2.2, timeval in corefiles has two 64 bit values. This
breaks gdb which expects two ints. Glibc 2.1 also uses 16 byte timevals,
breaking the old ping.
The solution is to compile gdb under glibc-devel-2.1 (Not sure about
that, but gdb from RH 6.0 works).
If you want to stay glibc2.0 only, inserting

struct timeval {
  long tv_sec;
  long tv_usec;
};
#define _STRUCT_TIMEVAL

before all the includes in bfd/elfcode.h (in the gdb sources) worked for
me.

When gdb is changed, old (2.0) coredumps are no longer readable.

HTH
- Alex



