  First off, check and make sure the drive works.  The Multia can have a 2.5"
SCSI hard drive in it, laptop size, which would be fairly quiet.  Some did come
with 3.5" drives, and most SCSI drives I've used have been as loud or louder
than IDE drives.  Once you are sure it spinning, you need to get the pathnames
right.
  The ARC console does use those funny NT style names, but they aren't identical
to NT's naming on x86.  Check the Menu's for a 'Display Hardware Setup' option,
and it should list all avalible drives and partitions.  First thing to try is to
boot milo off a floppy.  If you have a running Linux box and a RH CD, look in
milo/images on the cd, I think, for a image for the Multia, and use dd to write
it to floppy.
  Then add a boot menu item to boot it.  I'm at work so I can't check the
settings on my Alpha.  I'll post my setting from home later, or feel free to
e-mail me for more help.

