In article <wl7lp23zja.fsf@lem.phy.duke.edu>,
Henry S. Greenside <hsg@lem.phy.duke.edu> wrote:
>
>Can one easily run message-passing code on a dual-processor
>computer, e.g., a Compaq DS-20 or Linux equivalent? Or does
>one have to have truly two separate processors for this to
>work?

I don't know how well MPI works on Linux/Alpha on the
DS20, but I routinely use MPI on dual-CPU P-II machines
using Linux and it works well.  If you can get your message
passing to work between 2 DS10's it'll almost certainly work
better on a single DS20.

I personally would go for the dual machine, most especially
if it is $13k for the dual machine vs. 20k total for 2
DS10's, and I'd use the money I saved for more memory
and disk, Digital UNIX, and a better compiler.

However, I would also tend to want to run Digital UNIX
and not Linux:  the free compilers cannot compete yet,
in general, with DEC CC, Fortran, and CXX for producing
fast, highly optimized code.  The difference can be
substantial:  it is something like 30-40% on my own
numerical simulation code.  However, if you can benchmark
on Linux and Digital UNIX before buying, I highly encourage
you to do so.

Efforts at Compaq to port DEC CC and DEC Fortran to Linux
are underway, however, these projects are not complete
yet.

PeterM



