Ok, there were quite some people, who mailed me, so I took another look at
the Alpha: 

The daemons it runs (in order of CPU time):
  * rpc.nfsd
  * kswapd
  * actived
  * innwatch
  * Timm (this one monitors a PABX)
  * isdnlog
  
Besides that, there are some programs, which dynamically produce high loads
on time and memory:
  * postmaster
  * CGI scripts
  * mgetty and companions (converting G3 to GIF, 8-o *ugh!*)
  * CVS via ssh

IMHO the main reason, why the Alpha is so slow: it has 32M RAM and 256k
Cache only!

setiathome takes lots of RAM, sometimes 13-14M, which is nearly half of the
system memory! innd, actived, perl, postmaster, sshd and Timm take the rest
of the mem... :-/

So, it isn't suprising, that kswapd gets quite some CPU time...

Peter <MPe@pop.*.ch>, you said that your Multia 166MHz needs 40hrs *only*,
to process one SETI work unit, which is _half the time_ my NoName 166 needs!
Maybe you can give some more information about your configuration? Someone
else?

If someone could tell me, whether more memory & cache gives a speedup of
*TWO* (which marvels me, really...), I'd consider spending it another 32M
RAM and 1M cache...


Cheers,
Michael
BTW: originally, the Alpha was installed with Redhat 5.0...
