I am having a problem with swap memory in my Alpha/Linux system.

What's the correct behavior of the size of swap shown by "free"?

1.  Normally, it should be zero unless the system is in dire need 
   of virtual memory.  Or
2.  It may creep up to several MB, but it should go down quickly.  Or
3.  It may creep up to several MB.  It should stay that way forever
    as long as the machine is running.

On my machine LX-533MHz running 2.9.9-ac6 on RedHat-6.0, the behavior
shows the third one.  I think that stability of my system is 
closely linked to the size of the swap.  But, I can't find 
the offending place.  Maybe one of bugs in 
kernel, g77 (egcs-2.1.2), glibc-2.1, ...?

Symtoms include:
1. Swap increases forever.
2. Unknown floating-point error.  If I turn off swap, the same 
   program runs without a hitch.

Thank you for any information

Regards,

-- 
G. Hugh Song



