I just went through this with an AlphaServer 800. 2 things that are only
sort of in the manual:

1. Use BSD disk partitioning. If you need to wipe a disk, load the AlphaBios
(type "arc" at the >>> prompt), go into "Hard Disk Setup", create/format FAT
partitions and then blow then away immediately. Push in the halt button, and
reboot. Make sure to click the halt button off once you're at the >>>
prompt. Light up the RH install again (boot dka400 -file
kernels/generic.gz -flags "root=/dev/sdc0"), and when it comes to
patitioning, ignore DiskDruid and use fdisk. Press b, and you're in BSD
mode. It will prompt to create a BSD disk label, and let it. Next, create
your partitions (n command), but leave the c (whole disk) partition alone.
Then when it comes time to set the file system on each (t command), pick 1
(swap) for your swap, 8 (ext2) for your regular partitions and 5 (BSD 4.1)
for the whole disk partition (c). BTW, leave at least 5 Mb for the bootlx
program ( I left a whole cylinder, somy first partition started at cyl 2,
not one). Continue the rest of the install as normal.

2. Once the install is done and asking to reboot, press Alt-F2. At the #
prompt, type "chroot /mnt bash". This gives you a proper prompt, and you can
"/sbin/swriteboot /dev/sda bootlx -f3" - makes the disk bootable. Next, cd
to /mnt/boot, and "cp vmlinux-2.2.5-16  vmlinux" (makes for lless typing).
Make sure the permissions are set correctly on the copied file, then Alt-F1
to get back to the install screen, press in the halt button again, and press
enter to reboot. Again, click off the halt button at the >>> prompt, and
type the following:

>>>set boot_file 2/boot/vmlinux
(Note: the number is the partition number of root, on my system the 2nd ext2
partition)
>>> set bootdef_dev dka0
(Note: use the device number of your boot disk from the SRM devices command)
>>>set boot_osflags "root=/dev/sda2
(Note: use the device in /dev that is the partition number of root, on my
system the 2nd ext2 partition, so sda2)
>>> boot

should re-start the machine and take you all the way to Linux.

Hope this helps you out!

Ron Sokoloski
