> to (then) DEC C.  What's peculiar is that the bottleneck moved!
> With the DEC-compiled binary, the time-critical code was a banded
> matrix factorization routine.  With egcs, that routine was no longer
> the bottleneck.  The rest of the code is pretty convoluted, with lots
> and lots of pointers.

That's not peculiar at all. The ratio between egcs and Compaq's
compilers changes on different bits of code. If you're chasing
pointers, for example, the memory subsystem can bottleneck you more
than compiler quality.

