Tom Krotchko wrote:

> I bought a multia about 2-3 years ago, and just
> got it out of the box.  It worked back then.
>
> I fired it up, and I got the message:
>    Setting console to boot mode
>
> And the machine was basically hung up.
>
> I'd read about the sensitivity to a dead battery,

    I had exactly this problem, and replacing the battery fixed it.  I
used 3AA cells in a holder, since the stock battery is 4.5v and I didn't
want to risk using a higher voltage.  My Multia has been on for the past
two years.. looks like 2 yr. is the lifespan on the battery.

> This sounds like a batter problem, but the lack
> of video signal now has me concerned that the
> thing is so fussy I blew out something when I took
> off the battery (power was off though).

    The configuration data is stored in NVRAM, which is just
battery-backed SRAM.  (The firmware is kept in flash).  When you have a
dead batttery or no battery, the system doesn't know how it was
configured (including some of the SRM video options) and it sits there
and acts stupid.

    Note that once you put a new one in, it will boot to SRM, not ARC,
and your monitor may or may not sync to whatever mode the SRM video is
set to.  There are three (?) jumpers on the system board that determine
the mode.

-Jameel Akari




