Hi everyone,

I have problems with two Alpha  21164 PCs. We are running Redhat-Linux
5.2 with Kernel 2.2.5. Every few days trying to write in one (sometimes
several) of the directories results in a "filesystem full" message. At
the same time "df" reports that there is plenty of space.
This is what it looks like currently:
>df
Filesystem         1024-blocks  Used Available Capacity Mounted on
/dev/sda2             199271  131962    57018     70%   /
/dev/sda4            7899975 6865796   624631     92%   /usr

The problem appeared when the /usr partition was almost empty, so the
problem is not related to the space that is really available.
The only way to fix the problem is to reboot. At reboot fsck is run, and
it reports a lot of errors on the filesystem.
We are using a combined ethernet/scsi card called Netstorm 885. The
problem was there from day one. The machines were delivered with Kernel
2.0.? (the latest 2.0 Kernel). After the problem appeared a few times I
installed Kernel 2.2.5, which seemed to fix the problem at first, but it
still happens every ten days or so.

Has anyone else experienced a problem like this? Is there a way to fix
it?

Till Kuehner





