Reply-To: Paavo Hartikainen <pahartik@sci.fi>
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

I decided to post this message as I have not found any documentation about
getting CPML installed. Hopefully this helps someone. :)

To get Compaq Portable Math Library or general information about it, look
at 'http://www.unix.digital.com/linux/cpml.htm'.

After looking at various documents and asking around some, I managed to
get libcpml installed. It was not really all that difficult but I was not
even sure if 'ld' was the right tool for this. I have never used 'ld'
manually as 'gcc' or 'egcc' has done all that automatically. Another
completely new thing to me was 'ar'. If I did something wrong, please make
corrections. This is basically how I did it:

  mv cpml.0.1.tar.gz /usr/src/
  cd /usr/src/
  tar -xzvf cpml.0.1.tar.gz
  cd cpml.0.1/
  less README
  ar -x libcpml.a
  ld -shared -o libcpml.so *.o
  strip libcpml.so
  mv /lib/libm-2.0.7.so /lib/original_libm-2.0.7.so
  mv libcpml.so /bin/libm-2.0.7.so
  mv cpml.h /usr/include/


These are rough figures of performance increase that libcpml gives,
compared to libm performance. Test program was highly floating point
intensive (and it shows)... I wonder what this would look like if I had
256 KB of external cache installed, instead of none. CPU is DECchip 21066
at 166 MHz.

Performance of setiathome with libm:

 from 42.351471% to 43.795975% (=1.444504%)

  real    98m59.366s
  user    98m29.390s
  sys     0m8.154s

 from 46.461174% to 47.247684% (=0.78651%)

  real    54m49.109s
  user    53m53.102s
  sys     0m12.188s

Performance of setiathome with libcpml:

 from 43.795975% to 46.461174% (=2.665199%)

  real    87m50.483s
  user    86m36.263s
  sys     0m15.474s

 from 47.247684% to 48.941975% (=1.694291%)

  real    56m28.780s
  user    55m31.695s
  sys     0m12.246s


Ok, what does all this mean? Seti@home client was about twice as fast on
looking for data sent by someone out there, when libm was replaced by
libcpml.

--
