So then folks.  We have now had a week or so to try out the Alpha Linux
version of the seti@home client.  What do we think?

Most of us bought an Alpha in order to express our virility via stonking
floating point performance and seti@home is somewhere where this can really
shine.

Are we getting the performance which we expect?

It seems to me that the following questions would be worth trying to answer.

1) Do we have any idea who is responsible for compiling the Alpha Linux
version?  I presume that the seti@home folk have subcontracted it.

2) If we run the Alpha Linux version and the Alpha NT4 version on the same
hardware how does the speed compare?

3) Can anyone reverse engineer the code to deduce which math library has been
used (obviously, this is an application where libffm or the Compaq libm could
make a lot of difference)?

4) How do we feel the speeds being quoted by i386 users compares with what we
are getting?

5) Might it be worth seeing if we can get versions optimised for particular
Alpha chips?

For what its worth my 533 MHz LX164 takes an average of 11 hours 18 minutes.

Keep hunting those aliens, folks!

-- 
Richard Simpson
Farnborough, Hants, Uk                 Fax: 01252 392118
